        <div id="mah-vs-wh" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. mAh vs Wh: Compare High Capacity Power Banks in Watt-Hours
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every high capacity power bank is sold by milliamp-hours, and mAh is the least useful number on the box. It describes charge at the battery's internal voltage, so it only compares fairly between banks built on the same cells, and it cannot be compared with a laptop, a router or a power station at all. Watt-hours can. Converting is the first thing to do with any bank over 20,000mAh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Conversion</h3>

          <p>
            Watt-hours equal milliamp-hours times cell voltage, divided by 1,000. Most lithium-ion banks use cells around 3.6 to 3.7 volts. So a 20,000mAh bank holds about 72 to 74 Wh, a 27,650mAh bank about 99.5 Wh, and a 50,000mAh bank about 185 Wh. Better listings print the Wh figure directly; EcoFlow lists 99.54Wh, UGREEN 90Wh and Baseus 91.25Wh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why mAh Misleads on Big Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Some cheap banks quote mAh at an unstated voltage, or round wildly. A 50,000mAh claim on a pack that weighs under a pound should make you suspicious: lithium cells hold a fairly predictable amount of energy per gram, and a real 185 Wh bank weighs more than two pounds. If a listing will not state watt-hours and the weight looks too light, skip it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated vs Usable Energy</h3>

          <p>
            Expect about 60 to 70% of the rated watt-hours to reach your devices once voltage conversion and heat are counted. So a 99.5 Wh bank realistically delivers 60 to 70 Wh. That is the number to budget an outage with, not the label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated capacity to usable energy (about 65% delivered)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
