        <div id="outage-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Big a Power Bank for a Multi-Day Outage? Budget Watt-Hours Per Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right size depends on how many devices you keep alive and for how long. Build a simple daily energy budget, multiply by the days you want to cover, then divide by the usable energy of a bank. It takes five minutes and prevents both under-buying and lugging around far more battery than you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Estimate a Daily Budget</h3>

          <p>
            A modern smartphone battery holds roughly 13 to 18 Wh. In an outage you will use more than usual for news and messages, so budget one full charge per phone per day. A tablet holds about 25 to 40 Wh. A Wi-Fi router or fiber modem draws roughly 6 to 12 watts, which is 150 Wh or more over a full day, far more than any phone. A small LED lantern on USB uses a few watts an hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One Person, Phone Only</h3>

          <p>
            About 15 Wh a day. A 20,000mAh bank delivers around 48 Wh usable, so it covers roughly three days. That is why a single INIU or Anker Zolo is enough for most people living alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Family of Four, Phones and a Tablet</h3>

          <p>
            Four phones plus a tablet is about 90 Wh a day. A 27,650mAh bank's 65 Wh usable covers well under a day, so a family needs two or three large banks, or one large bank plus a way to refill it, to get through a weekend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Worked Example: Two Adults, Three Days</h3>

          <p>
            Two phones at about 15 Wh each is 30 Wh a day, or 90 Wh over three days. Add one tablet charge at around 30 Wh and the total is 120 Wh. Divide by roughly 65 Wh usable from a 27,650mAh bank and you need about two banks, or one large bank plus a way to refill it once. Two 20,000mAh banks at about 48 Wh usable each would fall a little short, which is why it pays to run the numbers rather than guess.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anything With a Router or a Wall Plug</h3>

          <p>
            Keeping internet up all day roughly doubles a family's daily budget, and anything with a two- or three-prong plug needs an AC outlet. Both point beyond a power bank to a portable power station, covered later in this guide. For the picks themselves, here they are side by side.
          </p>

