        <div id="output" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. PD Output Watts: 20W Charges Phones, 100W and Up Runs Laptops
          </h2>

          <p>
            Capacity says how long a bank lasts; output watts say what it can run and how fast. On a high capacity bank the output number is what separates a phone brick from a laptop-class battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phones and Earbuds: 20 to 30W</h3>

          <p>
            Phones fast-charge over USB Power Delivery at around 20 to 45W depending on the model. Anything above that is unused for a phone, so a phone-only kit does not need a 200W bank. The INIU and Anker Zolo cover this tier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tablets and Small Laptops: 45 to 65W</h3>

          <p>
            Tablets and thin laptops charge well at 45 to 65W. A 45W bank will keep a small laptop running and slowly charging; a 65W port charges most ultrabooks at full speed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Large Laptops: 100 to 140W</h3>

          <p>
            A 16-inch laptop can draw 100W or more. To charge it at full speed you need a 140W USB-C PD 3.1 port and a 5A cable rated for it. The Anker Prime, EcoFlow, UGREEN and Baseus all have 140W ports. If laptops are your main load, our <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank guide</a> goes deeper on matching watts to models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">USB-A and Low-Current Devices</h3>

          <p>
            Earbuds, fitness trackers and some headlamps draw so little current that a big bank may think nothing is connected and switch itself off. Many high capacity banks include a low-current or trickle mode, often triggered by double-pressing the power button; check the manual. A USB-A port is also handy for older lanterns, fans and radios that still ship with USB-A cables.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Total vs Per-Port Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Headline numbers like 250W or 300W are totals across all ports. Per-port limits are lower, and they drop further when several ports run at once. Check the per-port figure for the device that matters most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The 100 Wh Airline Limit: Why Most Big Banks Stop Near 27,000mAh
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest reason high capacity banks cluster around 25,000 to 27,650mAh is aviation rules, not chemistry. Manufacturers design right up to the limit so the bank can still fly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Up to 100 Wh: No Approval Needed</h3>

          <p>
            Under FAA and IATA guidance, power banks up to 100 Wh may be carried in carry-on bags without airline approval. A 27,650mAh bank at 3.6 volts is about 99.5 Wh, just under. That is why the Anker Prime and EcoFlow sit at exactly that capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">100 to 160 Wh: Airline Approval</h3>

          <p>
            Banks between 100 and 160 Wh need the airline's approval, and passengers are usually limited to two such spares. In practice few consumer power banks are sold in this band.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over 160 Wh: Not on Passenger Planes</h3>

          <p>
            Anything above 160 Wh, including the 185Wh Krisdonia and every portable power station, is forbidden on passenger aircraft. Buy one only if it will stay at home or in a vehicle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carry-On Only, and Print the Label</h3>

          <p>
            Spare lithium batteries must travel in carry-on bags, never checked. Make sure the Wh rating is printed on the bank; security staff may turn away an unlabeled pack. Some airlines also now restrict using banks during flight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pass-through" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pass-Through Charging: Useful for Outages, With Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pass-through means the bank can charge your devices while it is plugged in and recharging itself. For outage preparation it is the feature that lets a power bank sit on a nightstand, keeping itself and your phone full, then carry on seamlessly when the power drops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Picks List It</h3>

          <p>
            The Baseus 145W lists pass-through charging, and the Krisdonia enables it with a two-second press of the power button while charging. For other banks, check the listing or manual before relying on it, because some cut output briefly when input starts or stops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Caveats</h3>

          <p>
            Running power in and out at the same time makes heat, and heat is what ages lithium cells. Leaving a bank on pass-through 24 hours a day for years will wear it faster than charging it and unplugging it. The switchover is also not instant like a true UPS. For a desktop computer or network gear that must not blink, a <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">UPS battery backup</a> is the right tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Practical Middle Ground</h3>

          <p>
            Keep one bank on pass-through by the bed for phones during storm season, and keep a second bank fully charged and unplugged in a drawer. You get the convenience without wearing out your whole reserve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Fast Does a High Capacity Power Bank Recharge? Input Watts Decide
          </h2>

          <p>
            A large bank is only as useful as your ability to refill it. In multi-day outages you get short windows of power: an hour of generator time, a drive to work, a sunny afternoon. Input wattage decides how much of the bank you can refill in that window.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rough Math</h3>

          <p>
            Divide the bank's watt-hours by the input watts and add about 20% for losses and tapering. A 99.5 Wh bank on a 30W charger needs roughly four hours; on 140W, under an hour. EcoFlow's 320W input and Anker's 170W dual input are fast for exactly this reason, while the Zolo's 20W input means planning ahead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">From a Car</h3>

          <p>
            A good USB-C car charger rated 60W or more refills a large bank meaningfully during a 45-minute drive. Run the engine outside, never in an attached garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">From the Sun</h3>

          <p>
            A folding panel can top up a bank over a day, though output varies a lot with weather and angle. Our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank guide</a> covers panels and solar-charging banks that pair well with a high capacity pack for long outages at home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">From a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            If you run a generator a few hours a day, plug the banks in first thing. A fast-input bank fully refills before you shut the generator down. Inverter generators produce clean power that is safe for USB chargers; with an older open-frame generator, charge through a surge-protected power strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Own the Right Wall Charger</h3>

          <p>
            A bank's input rating only matters if your charger can supply it. Many people refill a 100W-input bank from a 20W phone brick and wonder why it takes all night. Keep a USB-C charger rated at or above the bank's input with your outage kit, along with a cable rated for that wattage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When to Step Up to a Portable Power Station: Wall Plugs and 200+ Wh
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank is the right tool for USB devices. At some point, though, the job outgrows it, and a portable power station is simply a bigger battery with AC outlets and higher input.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Need an AC Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            CPAP machines, fans, lamps, a fridge or a TV all have wall plugs. A power bank cannot run them. Small banks with an outlet exist, covered in our <a href="/best-power-bank-with-ac-outlet/">power banks with AC outlet</a> guide, but for anything beyond a CPAP or a laptop charger you want a station.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your Daily Budget Passes About 200 Wh</h3>

          <p>
            If your outage budget, with a router, a tablet and four phones, runs past 200 Wh a day, you would need three or four full-size banks to cover it. A single 300 to 1,000 Wh station is simpler, recharges from one cable or a panel, and shows a single reading of what is left.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Want Days, Not Hours, of Internet</h3>

          <p>
            Keeping a modem and router alive is the most common reason people outgrow banks. Running both all day can take 150 Wh or more, which drains a 27,650mAh bank in well under a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Start</h3>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a> ranks stations by capacity and outlets, and <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size power station you need</a> walks through the same watt-hour budget used here, scaled up to appliances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Storing a Power Bank for Emergencies: Half Charged, Cool, Checked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A high capacity bank bought for outages spends most of its life in a drawer. How you store it decides whether it is full and healthy when the lights go out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Around 50 to 60%, Top Up Before Storms</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion cells age fastest when stored completely full and warm. For long storage, leave the bank about half charged, then bring it to 100% when a storm is forecast. If you cannot predict outages, keep it full and accept slightly faster aging; a full bank in an emergency beats a perfectly preserved half-empty one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Cool</h3>

          <p>
            Avoid attics, hot garages and cars in summer. Room temperature in a closet is ideal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check It Every Three Months</h3>

          <p>
            All banks self-discharge slowly. Put a reminder in your calendar, check the level each season, and run the bank through one charge cycle a couple of times a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Retire Swollen or Damaged Banks</h3>

          <p>
            A bank that bulges, rattles, smells or gets very hot should be taken to a battery recycling drop-off, never thrown in household trash.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Banks by mAh Instead of Watt-Hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Milliamp-hours depend on cell voltage, so they only compare banks built on identical cells and say nothing about a laptop or router. Convert everything to watt-hours (mAh x 3.7 / 1,000), then plan on 60 to 70% of that reaching your devices. A bank that will not print its Wh rating is not one to rely on in an outage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a 50,000mAh Claim on a One-Pound Bank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium cells hold a fairly predictable amount of energy per gram. A genuine 50,000mAh, 185 Wh bank weighs more than two pounds. Listings that promise huge capacity in a tiny, light body, with no Wh figure and an unknown brand, routinely deliver far less. Check the weight and Wh rating before believing the number on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Bank Over 100 Wh and Expecting to Fly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Banks above 100 Wh need airline approval, and anything above 160 Wh, like a 185Wh home bank, is banned from passenger aircraft entirely. If the bank must travel, stay at or below about 27,650mAh and confirm the Wh rating is printed on the case. Keep bigger banks for the house and the car.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Input Watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A huge bank with a slow 18W or 20W input can take most of a day to refill, which is a problem in an outage where power returns only briefly. Check the input rating and own a wall charger that matches it. Fast-input banks refill in under an hour, so short generator or car windows actually restore your reserve.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Power Bank Where a Power Station Belongs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power banks run USB devices. Fridges, CPAPs, fans and anything with a wall plug need AC, and a family's full-day router budget quickly outgrows several banks. Once your daily budget passes about 200 Wh or you need an outlet, a portable power station is simpler and cheaper per watt-hour than stacking power banks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best high capacity power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Anker Prime 27,650mAh 250W. It holds about as much as airline rules allow, delivers 140W to a laptop and 250W in total, and Anker lists a full recharge in 37 minutes from 170W of input, which matters in outages with short windows of power. For phones only, the lighter INIU 20,000mAh 45W is better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the largest power bank allowed on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Without airline approval, 100 Wh, which is roughly 27,000mAh at 3.6 to 3.7 volts; that is why the Anker Prime and EcoFlow RAPID Pro stop at 27,650mAh. Banks from 100 to 160 Wh need the airline's approval and are usually limited to two per passenger. Anything above 160 Wh is forbidden on passenger aircraft. All power banks must go in carry-on bags, never in checked luggage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many days will a 20,000mAh power bank last in a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About three days for one person charging only a phone. A 20,000mAh bank holds about 74 Wh and delivers roughly 48 Wh after losses; a phone battery is about 13 to 18 Wh, and in an outage you should budget one charge per day. A family of four would drain the same bank in well under a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I convert mAh to Wh?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply the mAh by the cell voltage and divide by 1,000. Most power banks use cells at about 3.6 to 3.7 volts, so 20,000mAh is about 72 to 74 Wh and 27,650mAh about 99.5 Wh. Then expect 60 to 70% of that to reach your devices after conversion losses and heat. Better listings print the Wh rating directly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are 50,000mAh power banks real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but only from brands that state a realistic watt-hour rating and weight. The Krisdonia 50,000mAh lists 185Wh and weighs 1.2 kilograms, which is consistent with real cells. Many very cheap 50,000mAh-plus listings weigh under a pound and omit the Wh figure, which is a strong sign the capacity is overstated. Also note that 185 Wh cannot fly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is pass-through charging bad for a power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not occasionally, but constant use ages it faster. Charging and discharging at once creates heat, and heat wears lithium cells. Using pass-through by the bed during storm season is fine. Leaving a bank on pass-through year-round is not ideal, and the switchover is not as instant as a true UPS, so do not rely on it for a desktop computer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a power bank or a portable power station for outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A power bank for phones, earbuds, tablets and laptops; a portable power station once you need an AC outlet or your daily budget passes about 200 Wh. Routers, CPAPs, fans and fridges are station jobs. Many households keep both: a couple of 20,000mAh-plus banks for phones and a station for anything with a wall plug.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
